{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"plan","namespace":"","artifact_type":"","name":"P999000ed"},"owner_reference":"#3978","content":"{\"plan_id\":\"P999000ed\",\"feature\":\"primary-quota-403-retryable\",\"version\":\"1.0.0\",\"description\":\"\",\"goal\":\"Classify GitHub primary-quota rate-limit refusals as retryable with a bounded reset-derived retry_after on every REST and GraphQL path; align probe_backend_status with the same classifier (A); bound PyGithub's in-transport primary-limit wait (B); populate ViewItemResult.number (C); remove dead ViewItemResultCompact; keep permission-denied 403s, GraphQL refusals and secondary limits unchanged.\",\"context\":\"# Plan context: #3978 primary-quota 403 retryable\\n\\nInputs every task reads itself: `artifact_read(item_id=3978, artifact_type=\\\"architect\\\")` (spec, sections cited per task as 'spec \\u00a7N'), `artifact_read(item_id=3978, artifact_type=\\\"feature-context\\\")`, and `backlog_view(selector=\\\"#3978\\\", summary=False, sections=[\\\"Acceptance Criteria\\\"])` (12 ACs).\\n\\n## Test runner (verified 2026-09-28)\\nRun from the repo root: `uv run --locked --script plugins/development-harness/run_pytests.py <paths relative to plugin root> -q`. A bare `uv run pytest` from the repo root fails on this plugin's conftest import (`tests.network_blocked`). Loopback sockets are allowed by the plugin conftest network guard; all other network is blocked.\\n\\n## Planning decisions (auto mode)\\n- D1 reset-delay boundary: `rate_limit_reset_delay_seconds` returns `min((reset - now) + 1.0, 3900.0)`, and returns `None` when that value is <= 0, or when the header is absent, empty, non-numeric or non-finite. Consequence: reset == now gives 1.0; reset 1s or more in the past gives None. This settles the spec \\u00a76 'confirm exact boundary' note and the spec \\u00a72 Q1 / \\u00a74.1 wording mismatch.\\n- D2 (orchestrator): `requests.exceptions.RetryError` as retryable (spec \\u00a73.3) is its own task (T2) so review can judge it separately.\\n- D3 (orchestrator): a test pins PyGithub `GithubRetry().secondary_rate_wait == 60` and urllib3 `Retry.DEFAULT_BACKOFF_MAX == 120` as installed, and that the \\u00a79 threshold stays above both (T5). Observed values on 2026-09-28: 60 and 120.\\n- D4 (orchestrator): delete dead `ViewItemResultCompact` only after a search proves no reference (T7). This supersedes spec \\u00a77's Non-Goal on that class.\\n- D5 (orchestrator): Issue C is reproduce-first (T6 step 1 records failing output).\\n- D6: tests are written before or alongside each fix (TDD). Every task names its exact verification command.\\n\\n## Planning assumptions (unknown-frontier closures)\\n- U1 (GraphQL delay path in or out of scope): closed by spec \\u00a74.2a and AC9. In scope, T3.\\n- U2 (live primary-limit responses carry `x-ratelimit-reset` behind proxies): not verifiable offline, and running the live e2e lane locally against the production backlog is forbidden (AGENTS.md). Assumption: PyGithub passes response headers through unmodified (spec \\u00a73.2, Requester.createException). When the header is missing, the fix falls back to `retry_after=None` plus the sync engine's fixed 30/120s floor (spec Q1). No task. Residual risk is recorded for TN.\\n\\n## File ownership (same-file conflicts serialized by dependencies)\\n- sync_state.py: T1 then T2. tests/test_sync_state_classify.py: T1 then T2.\\n- gh_client.py: T3 only (GraphQL fallback and probe_backend_status merged).\\n- github_client.py and backlog_core/tests/test_github_client.py: T4 only. T5 writes a new file.\\n- parsing.py: T6. models.py: T7. backlog_core/ARCHITECTURE.md: T8.\\n- sync_engine.py, server.py and backends/github_backend.py are verify-only (T9). No task edits them.\\n\\n## Sync checkpoints\\n- SC1 (after T1): the classifier and delay helper are green, AC1-AC4 tests pass, and AC5-AC7 suites pass unmodified. Gates T2, T3 and T4.\\n- SC2 (after T2-T7): every structured-criterion suite passes, and `git diff` shows no edits to the verify-only files. Gates T8 and T9. Reflect: do the classifier (T1/T2), probe_backend_status (T3) and bounded retry (T4) agree on one rate-limit definition?\\n- SC3 (TN): baseline-versus-final comparison of the structured criteria.\",\"acceptance_criteria\":\"\",\"issue\":\"3978\",\"tasks\":[{\"id\":\"T0\",\"title\":\"T0: Capture baseline state\",\"status\":\"not-started\",\"agent\":\"t0-baseline-capture\",\"dependencies\":[],\"blocked_by\":[],\"parallelize_with\":[],\"priority\":1,\"complexity\":\"low\",\"skills\":[\"python-engineering:python3-testing\"],\"created\":\"2026-09-28T10:10:08.407349+00:00\",\"started\":null,\"completed\":null,\"last_activity\":null,\"body\":\"\",\"description\":\"\",\"analysis_method\":\"none\",\"divergence_notes\":0,\"accuracy_risk\":\"low\",\"is_bookend\":true,\"bookend_type\":\"t0-baseline\"},{\"id\":\"T1\",\"title\":\"sync_state: classify primary-quota 403 retryable and derive retry_after from x-ratelimit-reset\",\"status\":\"not-started\",\"agent\":\"python-engineering:python-cli-architect\",\"dependencies\":[\"T0\"],\"blocked_by\":[],\"parallelize_with\":[\"T6\",\"T7\"],\"priority\":1,\"complexity\":\"high\",\"skills\":[\"python-engineering:python3-testing\"],\"created\":\"2026-09-28T10:11:36.765222+00:00\",\"started\":null,\"completed\":null,\"last_activity\":null,\"body\":\"## Inputs\\n- Spec \\u00a72 (Q1, Q3, Q4, Q5), \\u00a74.1, \\u00a75 and \\u00a76 via `artifact_read(item_id=3978, artifact_type=\\\"architect\\\")`.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/sync_state.py` (`_classify_github_exception` ~351-379, `parse_retry_after_header`/`retry_after_seconds` ~412-460, `RETRYABLE_TRANSIENT_EXCEPTIONS` ~49, `__all__` ~57).\\n- Precedent: `gh_client._is_rate_limited` (~892-916) and PyGithub `GithubRetry.increment` (the installed `github/GithubRetry.py`).\\n\\n## CoVe Checks\\n- Claims to verify: (1) PyGithub assigns `RateLimitExceededException` only when `isRateLimitError` is true; (2) PyGithub lowercases header names on `GithubException.headers`; (3) GitHub's primary message starts with 'API rate limit exceeded'; (4) the frozen-clock tests actually control the clock the helper reads.\\n- Questions: Does `Requester.createException` in the installed `github/Requester.py` gate `RateLimitExceededException` on `isRateLimitError`? Does a test with `X-RateLimit-Reset` (mixed case) pass, proving the case-insensitive lookup? Does `str(None) == \\\"0\\\"` stay False for header-less exceptions, as the AC5 fixture requires? Does removing the monkeypatch make the exact-value tests fail (proving the freeze works)?\\n- Evidence: read the installed PyGithub source; pytest output.\\n- Revision rule: when any answer contradicts the spec, implement to the observed source behavior, and state the divergence in the handoff.\",\"description\":\"\",\"objective\":\"A 403 carrying any primary-quota signal classifies RETRYABLE, and `retry_after_seconds` returns a bounded delay derived from x-ratelimit-reset on 403/429 when Retry-After is absent. As a result the sync loop retries instead of going OFFLINE, and MCP errors report `retryable: true` with a `retry_after`.\",\"requirements\":\"### Tests first (TDD)\\n1. Write the failing tests below before editing production code. Record the red run output for the completion report.\\n2. `tests/test_retryable_classification.py`: add parametrized cases, each 403 with no Retry-After, asserting `retryable: true` and a positive `retry_after` at the MCP-response boundary (AC1, AC4): (a) `RateLimitExceededException(403, {\\\"message\\\": \\\"API rate limit exceeded for ...\\\"}, {\\\"x-ratelimit-remaining\\\": \\\"0\\\", \\\"x-ratelimit-reset\\\": <future epoch>})`; (b) a plain `GithubException` with the same message (signal 2 only); (c) a plain `GithubException(403, <unparseable body>, {\\\"x-ratelimit-remaining\\\": \\\"0\\\", \\\"x-ratelimit-reset\\\": <future>})` (signal 3 only).\\n3. `backlog_core/tests/test_rate_limit_retry_after.py`: add `rate_limit_reset_delay_seconds` cases with a frozen clock (monkeypatch the time source the helper reads; never assert against the real wall clock): reset=now+600 gives 601.0; reset=now gives 1.0; reset=now-1 gives None; reset=now-3600 gives None; header missing, `None` headers, `\\\"\\\"`, `\\\"abc\\\"`, `\\\"nan\\\"` and `\\\"inf\\\"` give None; reset=now+10**9 gives 3900.0; header-name casing (`X-RateLimit-Reset`) matches. Add `retry_after_seconds` cases: Retry-After wins when both headers are present (Q5); the reset fallback applies to 429 as well as 403 (Q4); a non-403/429 status gets no reset fallback (AC2).\\n4. `tests/test_sync_state_classify.py`: one case where a primary-limit `RateLimitExceededException` wrapped in `ContentUnavailableError` classifies RETRYABLE through the existing cause-chain walk (spec \\u00a76).\\n5. `backlog_core/tests/test_startup_sync.py`: one integration-style case (AC3). A primary-limit 403 raised inside `_attempt_sync` leaves `SyncStatus` not OFFLINE and sets `state.retry_after` to the reset-derived value. Use the existing `_attempt_sync` fixtures in that file.\\n\\n### Production change (`backlog_core/sync_state.py`)\\n6. Add `_PRIMARY_RATE_LIMIT_MESSAGE_PREFIX`, `_RATE_LIMIT_RESET_HEADER`, `_RATE_LIMIT_REMAINING_HEADER`, `_RATE_LIMIT_RESET_SKEW_SECONDS = 1.0` and `_MAX_RATE_LIMIT_RESET_DELAY_SECONDS = 3900.0` as `Final` constants with docstrings, per spec \\u00a74.1.\\n7. Add `_is_primary_rate_limit_signal(exc, message)`, true when any of the three signals holds (spec Q3), and OR it into the existing 403 `looks_retryable` expression. Keep the existing `has_retry_after` and secondary-marker terms unchanged.\\n8. Add public `rate_limit_reset_delay_seconds(headers)` implementing D1. Header lookup is case-insensitive, reusing the lookup approach of `parse_retry_after_header`. The function never raises on bad input.\\n9. In `retry_after_seconds`'s GithubException 403/429 branch, consult the new helper only when `parse_retry_after_header` returned None.\\n10. Add `\\\"rate_limit_reset_delay_seconds\\\"` to `__all__`. It is not there today, despite spec \\u00a74.1 saying 'already present'.\\n11. Update `classify_sync_error`'s classification-table docstring (~lines 504-543) with the primary-limit-signal bullet and the reset-delay fallback.\",\"constraints\":\"- `sync_state.py` must not import `gh_client` (the one-way `gh_client -> sync_state` direction).\\n- Do not reorder or alter the secondary-limit branch, the 5xx/429 rules, or any existing test assertion. AC5-AC7 suites must pass unmodified.\\n- Do not add `RetryError` handling (that is T2), and do not touch `gh_client.py`, `github_client.py`, `sync_engine.py`, `server.py` or `backends/`.\\n- No new Pydantic models, exception classes or schema changes.\\n- `git add` only the Expected Outputs. No `--no-verify`. No `Fixes/Closes/Resolves #N`.\",\"expected_outputs\":\"- plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/sync_state.py\\n- plugins/development-harness/tests/test_retryable_classification.py\\n- plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/test_rate_limit_retry_after.py\\n- plugins/development-harness/tests/test_sync_state_classify.py\\n- plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/test_startup_sync.py\",\"acceptance_criteria\":\"### Classification (AC1, AC4, AC5-AC7)\\n1. Each of the three primary-limit signals alone makes a 403 classify RETRYABLE, and the MCP verdict reports `retryable: true` with a positive `retry_after`.\\n2. `GithubException(403, \\\"Forbidden\\\", {})` stays NON_RETRYABLE. `test_startup_sync.py::test_403_without_retry_after_is_non_retryable`, `test_sync_state_secondary_rate_limit.py` and `test_sync_state_graphql_refusal.py` pass with zero diff to those tests.\\n### Delay (AC2)\\n3. Every D1 boundary case in Requirement 3 passes with the exact value listed. No case returns a negative number or a value above 3900.0.\\n4. Retry-After precedence and 429 coverage are pinned by tests.\\n### Sync loop (AC3)\\n5. The `_attempt_sync` case asserts status is not OFFLINE and `retry_after` equals the reset-derived delay.\\n### Hygiene\\n6. The red run was recorded before implementation. `__all__` exports the helper. The docstring table names the new rule.\",\"verification_steps\":\"1. Red: `uv run --locked --script plugins/development-harness/run_pytests.py tests/test_retryable_classification.py backlog_core/tests/test_rate_limit_retry_after.py tests/test_sync_state_classify.py backlog_core/tests/test_startup_sync.py -q` fails on the new cases before the production edit. Save the output.\\n2. Green: `uv run --locked --script plugins/development-harness/run_pytests.py tests/test_retryable_classification.py backlog_core/tests/test_rate_limit_retry_after.py tests/test_sync_state_classify.py backlog_core/tests/test_startup_sync.py tests/test_sync_state_secondary_rate_limit.py tests/test_sync_state_graphql_refusal.py backlog_core/tests/test_graphql_rate_limit_detection.py -q` passes.\\n3. `git diff --stat -- plugins/development-harness/tests/test_sync_state_secondary_rate_limit.py plugins/development-harness/tests/test_sync_state_graphql_refusal.py` is empty. `git diff plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/test_startup_sync.py` shows additions only.\\n4. `uv run ruff check plugins/development-harness/backlog_core && uv run ty check plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/sync_state.py`\\n5. `grep -n \\\"gh_client\\\" plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/sync_state.py` shows no import.\\n6. `git add plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/sync_state.py plugins/development-harness/tests/test_retryable_classification.py plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/test_rate_limit_retry_after.py plugins/development-harness/tests/test_sync_state_classify.py plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/test_startup_sync.py && git commit -m \\\"fix(backlog-core): classify primary-quota 403 retryable with reset-derived delay\\\"`\",\"context_notes\":\"Merged from two planned changes to one file (`sync_state.py`): (a) the 403 primary-limit classification signal and (b) the shared x-ratelimit-reset delay helper. They are one task because AC4 (MCP `retryable: true` with a `retry_after`) needs both. Spec \\u00a72 Q1/Q3/Q4/Q5, \\u00a74.1, \\u00a75 and the \\u00a76 rows for the test files below. Plan-context decision D1 fixes the delay boundary.\",\"handoff\":\"Report: the red and green outputs, the D1 values observed, confirmation that the AC5-AC7 test files have zero diff, and the commit SHA. Name any consumer whose behavior differed from spec \\u00a74.3.\",\"analysis_method\":\"none\",\"divergence_notes\":0,\"accuracy_risk\":\"high\",\"reason\":\"Primary edit site. Every consumer (sync engine, MCP verdict, backends, gh_client) routes through these two functions. T2, T3 and T4 build on it.\"},{\"id\":\"T2\",\"title\":\"sync_state: classify requests RetryError (exhausted transport retry budget) as retryable\",\"status\":\"not-started\",\"agent\":\"python-engineering:python-cli-architect\",\"dependencies\":[\"T0\",\"T1\"],\"blocked_by\":[],\"parallelize_with\":[\"T3\",\"T4\",\"T6\",\"T7\"],\"priority\":2,\"complexity\":\"low\",\"skills\":[\"python-engineering:python3-testing\"],\"created\":\"2026-09-28T10:12:48.102604+00:00\",\"started\":null,\"completed\":null,\"last_activity\":null,\"body\":\"## Inputs\\n- Spec \\u00a73.3 and \\u00a74.1 (the `RETRYABLE_TRANSIENT_EXCEPTIONS` block).\\n- Installed sources: `requests/adapters.py` (MaxRetryError to RetryError), `requests/exceptions.py`, and `github/GithubRetry.py` (`except (MaxRetryError, GithubException): raise`).\\n\\n## CoVe Checks\\n- Claims: (1) `RetryError` is a subclass of `OSError`; (2) `HTTPAdapter.send` raises `RetryError` when `MaxRetryError.reason` is a `ResponseError`; (3) `classify_sync_error` checks `RETRYABLE_TRANSIENT_EXCEPTIONS` before its generic OSError branch.\\n- Questions: Does `uv run python -c \\\"import requests; print(issubclass(requests.exceptions.RetryError, OSError))\\\"` print True? Does the installed `requests/adapters.py` contain the `isinstance(e.reason, ResponseError)` -> `RetryError` branch? Does placing the entry in the tuple actually change the verdict, or does an earlier branch intercept it (confirm with the red/green test)?\\n- Revision rule: when the branch order prevents the tuple from taking effect, stop and report BLOCKED with the observed order, rather than restructuring `classify_sync_error`.\",\"description\":\"\",\"objective\":\"`classify_sync_error(requests.exceptions.RetryError(...))` returns RETRYABLE through `RETRYABLE_TRANSIENT_EXCEPTIONS`. No other exception's verdict changes.\",\"requirements\":\"1. Test first: in `tests/test_sync_state_classify.py`, add a case asserting `requests.exceptions.RetryError(\\\"...\\\")` classifies RETRYABLE, and that `retry_after_seconds` on it returns None (no headers, per spec \\u00a73.3 and Q1 'no fabrication'). Record the red run.\\n2. Add `requests.exceptions.RetryError` to `RETRYABLE_TRANSIENT_EXCEPTIONS` (`backlog_core/sync_state.py` ~49-55), with a comment citing spec \\u00a73.3.\\n3. Add a `RetryError` bullet to `classify_sync_error`'s classification-table docstring.\\n4. Confirm that the tuple's other consumers (`grep -rn RETRYABLE_TRANSIENT_EXCEPTIONS plugins/development-harness/backlog_core`) are not changed in unintended ways, and list each consumer in the handoff.\",\"constraints\":\"- Only the tuple entry, its comment, the docstring bullet and the one test. No other edits to `sync_state.py`.\\n- Do not fabricate a `retry_after` for RetryError.\\n- `git add` only the Expected Outputs. No `--no-verify`. No `Fixes/Closes/Resolves #N`.\",\"expected_outputs\":\"- plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/sync_state.py\\n- plugins/development-harness/tests/test_sync_state_classify.py\",\"acceptance_criteria\":\"1. The new RetryError test fails before the edit and passes after it.\\n2. `retry_after_seconds(RetryError(...))` is None.\\n3. Every other case in `tests/test_sync_state_classify.py` and `backlog_core/tests/test_startup_sync.py` still passes. The completion report lists every consumer of `RETRYABLE_TRANSIENT_EXCEPTIONS` and whether its behavior changed.\\n4. The completion report explicitly flags this as the spec \\u00a73.3 addition beyond the item's ACs, for separate review.\",\"verification_steps\":\"1. `uv run --locked --script plugins/development-harness/run_pytests.py tests/test_sync_state_classify.py backlog_core/tests/test_startup_sync.py backlog_core/tests/test_rate_limit_retry_after.py tests/test_retryable_classification.py -q` (red before the edit, green after).\\n2. `grep -rn \\\"RETRYABLE_TRANSIENT_EXCEPTIONS\\\" plugins/development-harness/backlog_core`\\n3. `uv run ruff check plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/sync_state.py && uv run ty check plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/sync_state.py`\\n4. `git add plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/sync_state.py plugins/development-harness/tests/test_sync_state_classify.py && git commit -m \\\"fix(backlog-core): treat exhausted-retry RetryError as retryable\\\"`\",\"context_notes\":\"Spec \\u00a73.3: when PyGithub's `GithubRetry` exhausts `total`, urllib3 raises `MaxRetryError`, which requests converts to `requests.exceptions.RetryError` (an `OSError`, not a `GithubException`). `classify_sync_error` currently sends it to the generic OSError branch, which returns NON_RETRYABLE. This change is additive to `RETRYABLE_TRANSIENT_EXCEPTIONS`. It runs after T1 because both tasks edit `sync_state.py` and `tests/test_sync_state_classify.py`.\",\"handoff\":\"Report the red and green output, the consumer list from step 2 with a per-consumer impact note, the CoVe answers, and the commit SHA.\",\"analysis_method\":\"none\",\"divergence_notes\":0,\"accuracy_risk\":\"medium\",\"reason\":\"Orchestrator decision D2: spec \\u00a73.3 goes beyond the item's original ACs, so it gets a separately reviewable task and commit.\"},{\"id\":\"T3\",\"title\":\"gh_client: GraphQL reset-derived retry_after and probe_backend_status reuses classify_github_failure\",\"status\":\"not-started\",\"agent\":\"python-engineering:python-cli-architect\",\"dependencies\":[\"T0\",\"T1\"],\"blocked_by\":[],\"parallelize_with\":[\"T2\",\"T4\",\"T6\",\"T7\"],\"priority\":2,\"complexity\":\"medium\",\"skills\":[\"python-engineering:python3-testing\"],\"created\":\"2026-09-28T10:14:05.632505+00:00\",\"started\":null,\"completed\":null,\"last_activity\":null,\"body\":\"## Inputs\\n- Spec \\u00a74.2 and \\u00a78 (artifact `architect`, item 3978).\\n- `backlog_core/gh_client.py` (~76 import, ~892-916 `_is_rate_limited`, ~960-975 `_graphql_request`, ~2008-2050 `probe_backend_status`).\\n- `backlog_core/sync_state.py` after T1 (`rate_limit_reset_delay_seconds`, `classify_github_failure`, `_classify_github_exception` ~352-379).\\n- `tests/test_backend_status.py` `TestProbeBackendStatusCountFetchFailure` (~525-585).\\n\\n## CoVe Checks\\n- Claims: (1) `classify_github_failure` dispatches a `GithubException` to `_classify_github_exception`; (2) inside the `status == 403` guard, only the three 403 signals or Retry-After can yield RETRYABLE; (3) `_classify_github_exception` returns NON_RETRYABLE for 401 and 404 and RETRYABLE for 429 and status >= 500; (4) consumers of `BackendStatus` handle `BackendAvailability.ERROR` from this branch.\\n- Questions: Does `classify_github_failure` in `sync_state.py` (~463-475) reach `_classify_github_exception` for a bare `GithubException`? Does a 503 (RETRYABLE) in the new code reach the ERROR return rather than RATE_LIMITED, i.e. is the RATE_LIMITED path gated on status 429, or on status 403 plus the verdict? Does any consumer of `probe_backend_status` (grep the callers, including `backlog_core/server.py` `_probe_backend_status`) special-case REACHABLE-with-error or RATE_LIMITED in a way the new result would break? Does `GithubException(403, \\\"Forbidden\\\", {})` and each new non-403 case reach the issue-count `except` branch rather than the earlier `repo_obj is None` branch?\\n- Revision rule: when a consumer breaks on ERROR or RATE_LIMITED, report it in the handoff with file:line, and do not widen scope to that consumer.\",\"description\":\"\",\"objective\":\"A GraphQL primary-limit refusal with only x-ratelimit-reset raises `GitHubRateLimitedError` with a positive bounded `retry_after`. `probe_backend_status` derives its count-fetch failure availability from the status and one `classify_github_failure` call: RATE_LIMITED for a 429, or for a 403 the classifier marks RETRYABLE; ERROR for every other failure \\u2014 a plain permission-denied 403 and every other non-403 status (401, 404, 5xx), none of which is reported REACHABLE any more.\",\"requirements\":\"### Tests first\\n1. `backlog_core/tests/test_graphql_rate_limit_detection.py`: add a case (AC9) in which a GraphQL `GithubException` carrying `x-ratelimit-remaining: \\\"0\\\"` and a future `x-ratelimit-reset`, with no Retry-After, raises `GitHubRateLimitedError` whose `retry_after` equals `rate_limit_reset_delay_seconds(headers)` under a frozen clock. Add a case where Retry-After present still wins.\\n2. `tests/test_backend_status.py`: add cases (AC10, spec \\u00a76 row and \\u00a78.3): `RateLimitExceededException`/primary wording 403 gives RATE_LIMITED; secondary-marker 403 gives RATE_LIMITED; 403 with Retry-After gives RATE_LIMITED; `GithubException(403, \\\"Forbidden\\\", {})` gives ERROR (not RATE_LIMITED).\\n3. `tests/test_backend_status.py`, class `TestProbeBackendStatusCountFetchFailure` (AC10-ext): add `test_non_403_exception_returns_error`, parametrized with one case per status class \\u2014 `401` (id `unauthorized`), `404` (id `not_found`), `503` (id `server_error`). Each raises `GithubException(status=<n>, data={\\\"message\\\": ...}, headers={})` from `open_issues_count` and asserts `availability == BackendAvailability.ERROR`, `open_count is None`, and `error != \\\"\\\"`. Add `test_429_exception_returns_rate_limited`: `GithubException(status=429, data={\\\"message\\\": \\\"API rate limit exceeded\\\"}, headers={})` raised the same way gives `availability == BackendAvailability.RATE_LIMITED`, `open_count is None`, and `error != \\\"\\\"`. Delete `test_non_403_exception_returns_reachable` (its REACHABLE assertion is the behaviour being removed; the new 503 case replaces it). Record the red run of steps 1-3 before any production edit.\\n### Production change (`backlog_core/gh_client.py`)\\n4. Import `classify_github_failure` and `rate_limit_reset_delay_seconds` in the existing `.sync_state` import.\\n5. `_graphql_request` rate-limited branch (~965-967): when `parse_retry_after_header` returns None, fall back to `rate_limit_reset_delay_seconds(exc.headers)`.\\n6. `probe_backend_status` issue-count `except GithubException` branch (~2043-2049): return `BackendAvailability.RATE_LIMITED` with `error=str(exc)` when `exc.status == 429`, or when `exc.status == _HTTP_FORBIDDEN` and `classify_github_failure(exc) == SyncErrorKind.RETRYABLE`; return `BackendAvailability.ERROR` with `error=str(exc)` for every other status and verdict (NON_RETRYABLE 401/404, RETRYABLE 5xx, UNKNOWN). Remove the REACHABLE return from this branch. Call the classifier at most once.\\n7. Update `probe_backend_status`'s docstring to state this rule, and the `TestProbeBackendStatusCountFetchFailure` class docstring, which describes the REACHABLE rule.\",\"constraints\":\"- Do not modify `_is_rate_limited` or any other `gh_client.py` function (spec \\u00a77).\\n- Do not add a local re-derivation of the rate-limit or status signal in `gh_client.py`. Reuse `classify_github_failure` only.\\n- Do not add a new `BackendAvailability` member or change `sync_state._classify_github_exception`.\\n- Do not change consumers of `BackendStatus`. If one special-cases REACHABLE-with-error, report it with file:line instead.\\n- `git add` only the Expected Outputs. No `--no-verify`. No `Fixes/Closes/Resolves #N`.\",\"expected_outputs\":\"- plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/gh_client.py\\n- plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/test_graphql_rate_limit_detection.py\\n- plugins/development-harness/tests/test_backend_status.py\",\"acceptance_criteria\":\"### AC9 (GraphQL delay)\\n1. The new GraphQL case asserts `retry_after` is positive, at most 3900.0, and equal to the helper's value. The Retry-After-wins case passes.\\n### AC10 (probe_backend_status)\\n2. The plain `Forbidden` 403 gives ERROR. The primary, secondary and Retry-After 403s give RATE_LIMITED.\\n### AC10-ext (probe_backend_status non-403 statuses)\\n3. A count-fetch `GithubException` with status 429 gives `BackendAvailability.RATE_LIMITED`, and one with status 401, 404, or 503 gives `BackendAvailability.ERROR`; none gives REACHABLE, and each has `open_count` None and `error` non-empty. The 429 case is `test_429_exception_returns_rate_limited`; the 401, 404 and 503 cases are each their own case in `test_non_403_exception_returns_error`. All four are recorded failing before the production change. The rule is: RATE_LIMITED when the status is 429, or when the status is 403 and `classify_github_failure` returns RETRYABLE; ERROR for every other status and verdict.\\n### Non-regression\\n4. Every pre-existing case in both test files passes unmodified, except `test_non_403_exception_returns_reachable`, which is removed because AC10-ext supersedes it. `_is_rate_limited` has zero diff.\",\"verification_steps\":\"1. Red, then green: `uv run --locked --script plugins/development-harness/run_pytests.py backlog_core/tests/test_graphql_rate_limit_detection.py tests/test_backend_status.py tests/test_backlog_core_github.py tests/test_backlog_core_server.py -q`\\n2. AC10-ext red, then green, per status class: `uv run --locked --script plugins/development-harness/run_pytests.py \\\"tests/test_backend_status.py::TestProbeBackendStatusCountFetchFailure\\\" -v` \\u2014 the output names `test_429_exception_returns_rate_limited`, `test_non_403_exception_returns_error[unauthorized]`, `[not_found]` and `[server_error]`, failing before step 6 of Requirements and passing after.\\n3. `git diff plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/gh_client.py` touches only the import, `_graphql_request`'s rate-limited branch, and `probe_backend_status`; `grep -n \\\"BackendAvailability.REACHABLE, error\\\" plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/gh_client.py` returns no match.\\n4. `uv run ruff check plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/gh_client.py && uv run ty check plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/gh_client.py`\\n5. `git add plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/gh_client.py plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/test_graphql_rate_limit_detection.py plugins/development-harness/tests/test_backend_status.py && git commit -m \\\"fix(backlog-core): share reset delay with GraphQL and classify probe failures\\\"`\",\"context_notes\":\"Merged from two planned changes to `backlog_core/gh_client.py` to avoid edit conflicts: (a) AC9, where `_graphql_request` falls back to `rate_limit_reset_delay_seconds` when there is no Retry-After (spec \\u00a74.2a); (b) AC10 / Issue A, where `probe_backend_status` stops mapping every 403 to RATE_LIMITED and asks `classify_github_failure` instead (spec \\u00a78). Both extend the existing `from .sync_state import (...)` line (~76). Amendment by orchestrator decision: AC10-ext extends (b) to the non-403 branch, which previously returned REACHABLE-with-error for 401, 404, 429 and 5xx. 429 maps to RATE_LIMITED because GitHub uses it for both primary and secondary rate limits. Spec \\u00a78.2's keep-the-quirk instruction is superseded for this task.\",\"handoff\":\"Report the red and green output, the diff hunks summary from step 2, any other caller of `probe_backend_status` that reads RATE_LIMITED versus ERROR (`grep -rn \\\"RATE_LIMITED\\\\|BackendAvailability.ERROR\\\" plugins/development-harness`), and the commit SHA.\",\"analysis_method\":\"none\",\"divergence_notes\":0,\"accuracy_risk\":\"medium\",\"reason\":\"Merged: both changes are the only edits to gh_client.py (spec \\u00a74.2a and \\u00a78). Both consume T1's sync_state additions.\"},{\"id\":\"T4\",\"title\":\"github_client: bound PyGithub's in-transport primary-limit wait with _BoundedGithubRetry\",\"status\":\"not-started\",\"agent\":\"python-engineering:python-cli-architect\",\"dependencies\":[\"T0\",\"T1\"],\"blocked_by\":[],\"parallelize_with\":[\"T2\",\"T3\",\"T6\",\"T7\"],\"priority\":2,\"complexity\":\"high\",\"skills\":[\"python-engineering:python3-testing\"],\"created\":\"2026-09-28T10:15:24.924835+00:00\",\"started\":null,\"completed\":null,\"last_activity\":null,\"body\":\"## Inputs\\n- Spec \\u00a73.1, \\u00a73.2, \\u00a79 (artifact `architect`, item 3978).\\n- `backlog_core/github_client.py` (`make_github_client` ~912).\\n- Installed `github/GithubRetry.py` (`increment` ~92-200, static `get_content` ~211), `github/Requester.py` (`createException`), and `urllib3/util/retry.py`.\\n\\n## CoVe Checks\\n- Claims: (1) calling `self.get_content(response, url)` a second time in the subclass returns the same body that `GithubRetry.increment` already read (the response stream may be consumed); (2) urllib3 calls `Retry.sleep()` only after `increment()` returns, so raising inside `increment` means zero sleep; (3) the secondary branch's backoff is `max(secondary_rate_wait, retry.get_backoff_time())`, so it is at most 120 s under default `backoff_max` and stays below 150 s (spec \\u00a79.4 states only 60 s); (4) an exception raised from `increment` propagates out of requests unwrapped as the `GithubException` subtype.\\n- Questions: Does case (a) produce a non-empty `exc.data`/message (if not, claim 1 is false, so parse the body once and handle the consumed stream)? Does the E2E test's measured duration stay under 10 s? Does `GithubRetry.increment` for the secondary case ever return a backoff above 60 when `backoff_factor` is at its default? Does the E2E exception type equal `RateLimitExceededException`, not a wrapped `requests` error?\\n- Revision rule: when claim 1 fails, fix the body-reading approach and record the change against spec \\u00a79.2 in the handoff.\",\"description\":\"\",\"objective\":\"A REST call through `make_github_client()`'s client that meets a primary-limit 403 with a far reset raises `RateLimitExceededException` promptly, with the headers intact, which then classifies RETRYABLE with a reset-derived `retry_after`. Secondary-limit, 5xx and non-rate-limit 403 behavior stays unchanged.\",\"requirements\":\"### Tests first (`backlog_core/tests/test_github_client.py`)\\nPut every new test in a new class `TestBoundedGithubRetry`. None of them carries `@pytest.mark.e2e` or any other marker that `run_pytests.py`'s default `-m \\\"not e2e and not cross_backend and not integration and not research_vault\\\"` deselects (evidence in Requirement 3).\\n1. Unit cases on `_BoundedGithubRetry().increment(...)` with a fake urllib3 response (spec \\u00a76 row): (a) a primary-limit 403 (JSON body 'API rate limit exceeded...', no Retry-After, reset = now+3600) raises `RateLimitExceededException` whose `.headers` hold the `x-ratelimit-reset` and `x-ratelimit-remaining` values; (b) a secondary-limit 403 returns a Retry whose `get_backoff_time()` equals what plain `GithubRetry().increment` returns for the same input; (c) a 500 returns a Retry with backoff equal to plain `GithubRetry`'s; (d) a primary-limit 403 with reset = now+5 returns a Retry (no raise), proving the threshold is what gates the behavior; (e) a plain 'Forbidden' 403 raises the same exception type as plain `GithubRetry`.\\n2. Body-intact case (spec \\u00a79.2 open verification point), `test_raised_exception_keeps_response_body`. Write it and record its run before any production edit. Drive `_BoundedGithubRetry().increment(...)` with case (a)'s primary-limit 403 whose JSON body is `{\\\"message\\\": \\\"API rate limit exceeded for ...\\\", \\\"documentation_url\\\": \\\"...\\\"}`. Assert that the raised exception is `RateLimitExceededException` (not `json.JSONDecodeError`), that `exc.status == 403`, and that `exc.data` equals the parsed body. Asserting on `exc.headers` alone is not enough. Build the fake as a real `urllib3.response.HTTPResponse` over `io.BytesIO(body)` with `preload_content=False`. This matches how `requests.adapters.HTTPAdapter.send` calls `urlopen(..., preload_content=False)` (confirmed in the installed `requests`), so a second read of a consumed stream shows up instead of being hidden by a fake that serves the body again. If this test fails with the spec \\u00a79.2 code in place (empty or truncated body, or `JSONDecodeError`), apply the spec's named fallback: in `increment`, capture the body once from `response` before calling `super().increment(...)`, then pass that captured content to `Requester.createException` instead of calling `self.get_content` a second time. After the fallback, re-run cases (a)-(e) and confirm they still pass.\\n3. End-to-end case (AC11), `test_loopback_primary_limit_403_raises_within_bound`: start a stdlib `http.server` on 127.0.0.1 that answers every request with 403, body `{\\\"message\\\": \\\"API rate limit exceeded for ...\\\"}`, `x-ratelimit-remaining: 0` and `x-ratelimit-reset: <now+3600>`. Build a client with `make_github_client(token=\\\"x\\\", base_url=\\\"http://127.0.0.1:<port>\\\")`, make one REST call (for example `get_repo(\\\"o/r\\\")`), and assert all of: it raises `RateLimitExceededException` within 10 s wall clock; `classify_github_failure(exc) == SyncErrorKind.RETRYABLE`; `retry_after_seconds(exc)` is in (3500, 3900]; `exc.data[\\\"message\\\"]` equals the server's message (the body survives the real transport path). Record the red run. Before the fix the call must time out or block, so bound the red run with pytest-timeout or a thread join, and do not let it hang the suite. Do not mark this test `e2e`, and do not put it in `TestAgainstLiveGitHub`. Evidence: the plugin `conftest.py` (module docstring, and `_LOOPBACK_HOSTS` ~line 42) allows loopback connections in every non-e2e test, and `backlog_core/tests/test_graphql_rate_limit_detection.py` already runs an unmarked `HTTPServer((\\\"127.0.0.1\\\", 0), ...)`. The only `@pytest.mark.e2e` in `test_github_client.py` (~line 1039) is on `TestAgainstLiveGitHub`, which needs the live network, not on a loopback test.\\n### Production change (`backlog_core/github_client.py`)\\n4. Add `_MAX_INLINE_RATE_LIMIT_WAIT_SECONDS: Final = 150.0` with the spec \\u00a79.2 docstring naming the 60 s and 120 s couplings, plus `_BoundedGithubRetry(GithubRetry)` overriding `increment` per spec \\u00a79.2 (or with the capture-once fallback from Requirement 2 if the body-intact test requires it).\\n5. Pass `retry=_BoundedGithubRetry()` in `make_github_client`'s `Github(...)` call. Make no other constructor change.\",\"constraints\":\"- Do not change `total`, `status_forcelist`, `secondary_rate_wait`, `backoff_factor` or `backoff_max`. The subclass takes no constructor arguments.\\n- Do not touch `sync_state.py`, `gh_client.py` or any other file outside the Expected Outputs.\\n- The E2E test must not use non-loopback network, must not sleep for real rate-limit windows, and must not hang the suite on failure.\\n- `git add` only the Expected Outputs. No `--no-verify`. No `Fixes/Closes/Resolves #N`.\",\"expected_outputs\":\"- plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/github_client.py\\n- plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/test_github_client.py\",\"acceptance_criteria\":\"### Bounded wait (AC11)\\n1. `test_loopback_primary_limit_403_raises_within_bound` raises within 10 s. The exception classifies RETRYABLE, `retry_after_seconds` is in (3500, 3900], and `exc.data[\\\"message\\\"]` is the server's message. The test has no e2e marker and appears as `PASSED backlog_core/tests/test_github_client.py::TestBoundedGithubRetry::test_loopback_primary_limit_403_raises_within_bound` in the default runner's `-rA` summary, which proves the default `-m` filter does not deselect it.\\n2. Case (a) raises with the original headers present on `exc.headers`.\\n### Body intact (spec \\u00a79.2)\\n3. `test_raised_exception_keeps_response_body` was written and run before the production edit. After the edit it passes: `exc.data` equals the parsed body, `exc.status == 403`, and no `JSONDecodeError` is raised. It appears as `PASSED backlog_core/tests/test_github_client.py::TestBoundedGithubRetry::test_raised_exception_keeps_response_body` in the `-rA` summary. If the spec \\u00a79.2 second-read code failed this test, the shipped code uses the capture-once fallback, and the handoff records that divergence.\\n### Preservation (spec \\u00a79.4, \\u00a79.5)\\n4. Cases (b) and (c) show backoff values numerically equal to plain `GithubRetry`'s. Case (d) returns a Retry. Case (e) raises the same type as plain `GithubRetry`.\\n5. Every pre-existing test in `test_github_client.py` passes unmodified.\",\"verification_steps\":\"1. Red, then green: `uv run --locked --script plugins/development-harness/run_pytests.py backlog_core/tests/test_github_client.py -q -rA`. Bound the red run as Requirement 3 describes. On the green run, the exit code is 0 and both of these print a line: `uv run --locked --script plugins/development-harness/run_pytests.py backlog_core/tests/test_github_client.py -q -rA | grep -F \\\"PASSED backlog_core/tests/test_github_client.py::TestBoundedGithubRetry::test_loopback_primary_limit_403_raises_within_bound\\\"` and the same command with `grep -F \\\"PASSED backlog_core/tests/test_github_client.py::TestBoundedGithubRetry::test_raised_exception_keeps_response_body\\\"`. The step fails if either PASSED line is missing, including when the test was deselected or skipped.\\n2. Adjacent suites: `uv run --locked --script plugins/development-harness/run_pytests.py backlog_core/tests/test_gh_client_auth.py tests/test_backlog_core_github.py tests/test_backend_status.py -q`\\n3. `uv run ruff check plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/github_client.py && uv run ty check plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/github_client.py`\\n4. `git add plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/github_client.py plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/test_github_client.py && git commit -m \\\"fix(backlog-core): bound PyGithub primary rate-limit wait per REST call\\\"`\",\"context_notes\":\"Spec \\u00a73.1, \\u00a73.2, \\u00a79. `make_github_client` (`backlog_core/github_client.py` ~912) passes no `retry=`, so `Github.default_retry = GithubRetry()` sleeps `(reset - now) + 1` seconds synchronously on a primary-limit 403. The fix is a `GithubRetry` subclass that raises `Requester.createException(...)` when the computed backoff exceeds `_MAX_INLINE_RATE_LIMIT_WAIT_SECONDS = 150.0`. The AC11 end-to-end assertion (retryable plus `retry_after`) uses T1's classifier, hence the dependency on T1.\",\"handoff\":\"Report the red and green output, the wall-clock duration of the E2E case, the CoVe answers (especially Q1), any divergence from spec \\u00a79.2's code, and the commit SHA.\",\"analysis_method\":\"none\",\"divergence_notes\":0,\"accuracy_risk\":\"high\",\"reason\":\"Issue B (AC11). Without it, REST primary-limit refusals block for up to an hour inside PyGithub before any classifier runs.\"},{\"id\":\"T5\",\"title\":\"Pin upstream GithubRetry/urllib3 defaults that the 150s bounded-retry threshold depends on\",\"status\":\"not-started\",\"agent\":\"python-engineering:python-pytest-architect\",\"dependencies\":[\"T0\",\"T4\"],\"blocked_by\":[],\"parallelize_with\":[\"T2\",\"T3\",\"T6\",\"T7\"],\"priority\":3,\"complexity\":\"low\",\"skills\":[\"python-engineering:python3-testing\"],\"created\":\"2026-09-28T10:16:34.923649+00:00\",\"started\":null,\"completed\":null,\"last_activity\":null,\"body\":\"## Inputs\\n- Spec \\u00a79.2 and \\u00a79.4 (artifact `architect`, item 3978).\\n- `backlog_core/github_client.py` after T4.\\n- Installed `github/GithubRetry.py` (`DEFAULT_SECONDARY_RATE_WAIT`) and `urllib3/util/retry.py` (`DEFAULT_BACKOFF_MAX`).\\n\\n## CoVe Checks\\n- Claims: (1) `GithubRetry().secondary_rate_wait` is 60 in the locked environment; (2) `Retry.DEFAULT_BACKOFF_MAX` is 120; (3) `GithubRetry` instances expose `backoff_max`.\\n- Questions: Does `uv run python -c \\\"import github; print(github.GithubRetry().secondary_rate_wait, github.GithubRetry().backoff_max)\\\"` print `60 120`? Note that `import github.GithubRetry as m` binds the class, not the module; use `importlib.import_module(\\\"github.GithubRetry\\\")` when module constants are needed.\\n- Revision rule: when observed values differ from 60/120, stop and report BLOCKED with the observed values. The \\u00a79 threshold then needs re-deriving by the architect.\",\"description\":\"\",\"objective\":\"A dedicated test fails whenever the installed PyGithub secondary wait or urllib3 backoff cap changes, or whenever `_MAX_INLINE_RATE_LIMIT_WAIT_SECONDS` is no longer strictly above both.\",\"requirements\":\"1. Create `backlog_core/tests/test_upstream_retry_defaults.py` with behavior-named tests asserting: `GithubRetry().secondary_rate_wait == 60`; `Retry.DEFAULT_BACKOFF_MAX == 120`; `_BoundedGithubRetry().backoff_max == Retry.DEFAULT_BACKOFF_MAX` (the subclass does not override it); `_BoundedGithubRetry().secondary_rate_wait == GithubRetry().secondary_rate_wait`; and `_MAX_INLINE_RATE_LIMIT_WAIT_SECONDS > max(secondary_rate_wait, backoff_max)`.\\n2. Each assertion message names spec \\u00a79.4 and says to re-derive the threshold when it fails.\\n3. Read the values from the installed libraries at test time. Do not copy them into constants other than the expected literals 60 and 120.\",\"constraints\":\"- Only the new test file. Do not edit `github_client.py` or `test_github_client.py`.\\n- Do not use the network.\\n- `git add` only the Expected Outputs. No `--no-verify`. No `Fixes/Closes/Resolves #N`.\",\"expected_outputs\":\"- plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/test_ups